{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgtPPW8f8EkVntMGtG93w","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I added the `codex-sandboxed` lane: `./run.sh <task> codex-sandboxed [rep]`. It runs the same `codex exec` command as the `codex` lane, wrapped the way `kimi` is: a scratch HOME in `/tmp` plus `sandbox-exec -f sandbox/agent.sb`. I tested it with a stand-in `codex` script, not a real Codex run, because a real run sends the task to OpenAI.\n\n**What the test showed:** I ran the full `./run.sh fizz codex-sandboxed` with the stand-in on PATH. Inside the sandbox it couldn't read `~/.codex` or `~/.kimi-code` and couldn't write to `~`. It could write to its scratch HOME and the task folder. The run produced `final.txt`, `answer.json` read it, and the exit code was 0. The real `codex --version` also runs fine under the profile. One real run on the eval Mac (`./run.sh fizz codex-sandboxed`) would confirm the rest.\n\nTwo changes went beyond adding the lane:\n\n- **`sandbox/agent.sb` change:** the one folder the profile opens was hard-coded to `~/.kimi-code`. Used as-is, the Codex agent could have read Kimi's install and credentials and sent them to OpenAI. That folder is now a parameter, `-D AGENT_DIR=...`. The `kimi` lane passes `~/.kimi-code`, so its rules are the same as before (I checked it still opens that folder and still blocks `~/.codex`). The new lane passes its scratch HOME, so nothing in the real home is opened.\n- **Where `final.txt` gets written:** Codex writes this file itself, from inside the sandbox. On the eval Mac `runs/` is under the real home, so a direct write would be blocked. Codex writes it to a scratch folder and `run.sh` copies it into the run folder afterwards.\n\n**Differences from the `codex` lane:**\n- **No user config:** `codex` loads `~/.codex/config.toml`, which on this machine starts the pencil and posthog MCP servers and plugins. The sandboxed lane starts with an empty config, so it runs without them. That config doesn't set a model, so the model should be the same.\n- **Codex's built-in sandbox is still off**, as in `codex`, because macOS sandboxes can't run inside one another. `agent.sb` is the fence, and network access stays open, as it does for `kimi`.\n\nI also added the lane to `README.md`. The details are in `ANSWER.md`. Nothing is committed."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1703,"cache_read_input_tokens":32590,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1703},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b6a7a64f-707d-4b72-a487-ffbe13343a51","uuid":"407d413a-5943-442b-98f5-a5b681d8b6de","timestamp":"2026-11-10T01:33:55.389Z","request_id":"req_011CgtPPVzycYeTTpiNVhsJh"}
